WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.106
Constructive problems ask you to build any valid configuration or prove that none exists.

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:15.299
Before searching for a pattern, test simple invariants like total sum parity, degree bounds, and minimum or maximum achievable values.

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:22.640
Outputting negative one immediately on impossible bounds avoids complex edge-case bugs inside your builder.

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:33.654
When asked for permutations with large or distinct adjacent differences, interleave the smallest and largest remaining numbers: one, N, two, N minus one.

00:00:33.654 --> 00:00:43.197
On grids, a boustrophedon snake path visits every cell in a single continuous chain that you can chop into pieces of any required sizes.

00:00:43.197 --> 00:00:46.917
Standard canonical shapes eliminate ad-hoc guesswork.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:58.820
Powers of two one, two, four, up to two to the k, together with the remaining difference R, can form every integer sum from one to N.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:09.860
Similarly, decomposing a graph into a star or a simple chain gives full control over diameter, leaf count, or degree sequence.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.767
Reduce N-variable freedom down to one tunable parameter.

00:01:15.042 --> 00:01:21.880
Because constructive problems often allow many valid outputs, sample tests rarely catch subtle boundary flaws.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:29.652
Write a five-line Python verifier that checks all problem rules on your generated output and fuzz it across all small inputs.

00:01:29.652 --> 00:01:33.693
Catching a broken invariant locally saves twenty penalty minutes.
